{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Guessing about the present",
      "body": "Spanish uses the simple **future** to make guesses about the **present** — like English \"must be\", \"probably\", or \"I wonder\":\n\n- *¿Qué hora será?* — I wonder what time it is.\n- *Serán las ocho.* — It must be about eight.\n- *¿Dónde estará Juan?* — Where could Juan be?\n- *Estará en el trabajo.* — He's probably at work.\n- *Tendrá unos cuarenta años.* — She must be about forty.\n\nThis is extremely common in conversation, and learners often miss it. When you hear the future in a question with no future time reference, it's usually a guess."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Future forms: a quick review",
      "body": "Remember the regular future adds *-é, -ás, -á, -emos, -án* to the infinitive, and the irregular stems: *tendr-, pondr-, saldr-, vendr-, podr-, sabr-, querr-, har-, dir-, habr-*. So: *¿Quién será? Habrá mucha gente. No sabrá la respuesta. Querrá ir.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Other ways to guess",
      "body": "*Debe (de) + infinitive* (must be): *Debe de estar cansado.* *Seguramente / probablemente* + present: *Seguramente está en casa.* *Supongo que…* (I suppose that…): *Supongo que tienes hambre.*"
    }
  ],
